{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Every firstborn.</b> Whether firstborn to the father or firstborn to the mother." "<b>From the firstborn of Pharaoh.</b> This is firstborn to the father; until the firstborn of the maidservant — this is firstborn to the mother." "<b>Until the firstborn of the maidservant.</b> This is the reading of perush HaShem R. Yeshaya, for in most of the versions he adopts here 'until the firstborn of the captive.' And some weigh the opinion of perush HaShem R. Yeshaya: the firstborn of the maidservant is the firstborn of the captive, for a female captive is a maidservant. 'Until the firstborn of the maidservant' — but at the completion of the plague he says, 'until the firstborn of the captive.' Rather, when the firstborns heard that Moses said 'until the firstborn of the maidservant,' they rejoiced and said, 'Let us go into captivity and not die, for the firstborn of the captive is less than the firstborn of the maidservant.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I said 'until the firstborn of the maidservant' — given that they still seek to flee, I will add to strike them 'until the firstborn of the captive.' Another interpretation: <b>until the firstborn of the captive</b> — he is included in the firstborn of the maidservant, for the prophets do not attend to the poetic expressions but to the reasons, as in the poetic expression השקיני נא הגמיאיני, שמור וזכור, שוא ושקר, לא חמד ולא תתאוה — they are one reason. Another interpretation: when Moses said <b>and every firstborn shall die</b>, the firstborns of the maidservants rejoiced and said: now there will be no distinction between us and our masters. When their masters saw that they rejoiced at their downfall, they bound them in the pit, and the plague came upon them while they were still in the pit. It turns out that at the time of the warning it was 'firstborn of the maidservant,' and at the time of the plague, 'firstborn of the captive.'" "<b>Who is behind the mill.</b> But at the completion of the plague he says, בבית הבור — rather, it is as we have said. And one may say that these two verses are one, for the firstborn of the captive is in the house behind the mill, as we find with Samson: ויהי טוחן בבית האסורים — and at night they would put them in the pit and place the mill over the mouth of the pit. Thus 'behind the mill' is like בבית הבור." "<b>And every firstborn of animal.</b> Perush HaShem R. Yeshaya: the Holy One, blessed be He, exacts retribution from a nation and afterwards from its deities. This is correct, for in the section of Va'era, regarding ונהפכו לדם, perush HaShem R. Yeshaya said: He struck their river and afterwards struck them."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:05.593570934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}